WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.000
How do user threads switch

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.480
stacks? A user-thread library can

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.780
save one thread execution context

4
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.360
and restore another while the

5
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.300
kernel still schedules their containing

6
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.760
process. In the lab many-to-one

7
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.920
model, these threads share one

8
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.380
kernel scheduling context.

9
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.480
Each user thread needs its

10
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.040
own stack and saved register

11
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.320
context. Threads share process code

12
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.160
and ordinary global data, so

13
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.780
switching stacks does not isolate

14
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.840
shared memory. Keep the selected

15
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.320
thread identity, its state and

16
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.580
its stack pointer consistent.

17
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.140
For a suspended thread, saving

18
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.780
the stack pointer preserves where

19
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.340
its call frames live, while

20
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.260
the return address and callee-saved

21
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.680
registers preserve the resumption path.

22
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.060
Restore those values before returning

23
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.480
into that thread. Merely changing

24
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.640
a current-thread pointer does not

25
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.540
move execution onto its stack.

26
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.520
A new thread has no

27
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.220
suspended call frame to resume.

28
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.580
Initialize its return path to

29
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.200
an appropriate entry or startup

30
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.740
routine and its stack pointer

31
00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.880
to the usable top of

32
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.600
its own stack. The startup

33
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.980
path must handle the thread

34
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.160
function finishing rather than returning

35
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.440
into an undefined caller.

36
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.800
The manual uses the standard

37
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.400
sixteen-byte stack-alignment requirement for the

38
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.960
RISC-V call boundary. Align the

39
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.700
initial stack top accordingly and

40
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.360
preserve that requirement through the

41
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.460
startup layout. Extra bytes of

42
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.060
storage alone do not establish

43
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:21.780
correct alignment.

44
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.440
A cooperative thread calls thread

45
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.840
yield or exits to let

46
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.520
the library select another runnable

47
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.420
thread. If it never yields

48
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.700
or blocks through a supported

49
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.780
library path, its peers may

50
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.120
not progress. A blocking kernel

51
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.560
call can suspend the whole

52
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.720
containing process in this many-to-one

53
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:39.080
model.

54
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.880
Trace one round with A

55
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.260
yielding to B and B

56
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.300
yielding back to A. Each

57
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.800
resumes at its own saved

58
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.080
return path with its own

59
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.560
stack contents. Check thread states

60
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.960
and shared counters as well

61
00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.660
as printed output, because a

62
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.420
plausible print sequence alone does

63
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.840
not prove a correct context

64
00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.600
implementation.

65
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.340
Things to take note of.

66
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.720
Private stacks and saved contexts

67
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.220
enable resumption, while shared data

68
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.240
still needs coordination. Initialize new

69
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.220
threads differently from suspended ones,

70
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.440
respect sixteen-byte alignment, and distinguish

71
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.580
cooperative user scheduling from kernel

72
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.080
preemption.
